@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s

@title Presentation

@s From Safe Page Bodies to Complete Offline Output

Weaver produced safe linked page bodies, and Book Weaving decided which pages
a book needs. Presentation asks what surrounds those bodies so they remain
readable and useful offline: a complete HTML shell, an embedded color scheme,
and optional local support for equations and syntax highlighting.

Here is the complete input that will carry the explanation:

````text
@code_type rust .rs
@comment_type // %s
@title Distance

@s Squared Distance
The formula $d^2 = x^2 + y^2$ gives the squared distance.
The implementation is in @{distance.rs}.

--- distance.rs
fn squared_distance(x: f64, y: f64) -> f64 {
    x * x + y * y
}
---
````

We will weave this example with its identifier index disabled, so the page
contains only the material needed here. Weaver has already made the body safe:
the named-block reference is a link to section 1, the TeX source is escaped
inside a marked math span, and the Rust source is escaped inside a code
element. The complete `main` element is:

```html
<main>
<h1>Distance</h1>
<section class="section" id="1:1">
<div class="section-opening">
<h2 class="section-heading"><span
 class="section-number">1.</span> <span
 class="section-title">Squared Distance</span>.</h2>
<p>The formula <span
 class="litweb-math litweb-math-inline">d^2 = x^2 + y^2</span> gives the
squared distance.
The implementation is in <code
 class="section-reference">⟨distance.rs <a
 href="#1:1" aria-label="section 1">1</a>⟩</code>.</p>
</div>
<div class="codeblock">
<div class="codeblock_name"><span
 class="section-name">⟨<strong>distance.rs</strong> <a
 href="#1:1" aria-label="section 1">1</a>⟩</span> <span
 class="definition-operator" aria-label="is defined as">≡</span></div>
<pre class="language-rust"><code
 class="language-rust" data-litweb-highlight="pending"
 data-litweb-language="rust rs">fn squared_distance(x: f64, y: f64) -&gt; f64 {
    x * x + y * y
}
</code></pre>
</div>
</section>
</main>
```

Presentation completes `distance.html`, notices the two kinds of marked
source, and adds the corresponding offline bundles after the page:

```text
WeavePlan
|-- distance.html
|-- litweb-assets/katex-0.18.1/...
`-- litweb-assets/prism-1.30.0/...
```

The page is therefore useful in two stages. Its source fallbacks work as soon
as the HTML opens. When local scripts are available, KaTeX replaces the first
fallback with a typeset equation and Prism decorates the second with syntax
colors. We will first complete the page shell, then follow each enhancement
from fallback marker to planned asset directory.

@s Choosing Optional Features

The opening `Distance` example uses these options to omit its identifier
index while retaining Litweb's default highlighting scheme. In general,
identifier indexes and highlighting are enabled by default.
@code{plan_weave} provides those defaults. @code{plan_weave_with_options}
lets a command-line or library caller suppress the index or select a color
scheme for every page in this particular plan.

--- Weave options
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct WeaveOptions {
    pub identifier_index: bool,
    pub color_scheme: Option<PathBuf>,
}

impl Default for WeaveOptions {
    fn default() -> Self {
        Self {
            identifier_index: true,
            color_scheme: None,
        }
    }
}
---

@s Facts Shared While Rendering

The resolved block graph, shared presentation layout, and relationship index
are three read-only views of the same program. @code{WeaveData} keeps them
together while HTML pages are rendered. @code{RenderContext} adds the facts
that can change from one page to another.

For example, a link renderer needs the current page path and chapter to decide
whether `#1:1` is enough or whether it must first navigate to another file.
@code{single_page} distinguishes one combined document from a book, and
@code{highlighting} decides whether target-code elements should request later
browser work. A book contents page has no current chapter; a one-file page and
each chapter page do.

--- Page render context
struct WeaveData<'a> {
    resolved: &'a ResolvedProgram,
    layout: &'a WeaveLayout,
    index: &'a WeaveIndex,
}

struct RenderContext<'a> {
    current_chapter: Option<usize>,
    current_path: &'a Path,
    single_page: bool,
    highlighting: bool,
    book_paths: Option<&'a BookHtmlPaths>,
}
---

@s Choosing a Page's Color Scheme

A color scheme supplies the syntax colors for one whole page, even if its
sections contain several target languages. The `Distance` page has no
`@colorscheme` command, so it receives Litweb's bundled light scheme.
@code{PageColorScheme} represents that choice as disabled, built in, or the
text of a user-selected CSS file.

Four levels determine the result, from strongest to weakest:

1. a command-line selection applies to every page;
2. a chapter's `@colorscheme` overrides its book manifest;
3. the manifest supplies the choice for other chapters and the contents page;
4. with no selection, Litweb uses its default scheme.

An empty `@colorscheme` argument or the name `none` disables highlighting.
Built-in names are case-insensitive. A relative CSS path written in a `.lit`
file begins beside that file, while a command-line path begins in the
process's current directory. Reading a selected custom file is the one
intentional filesystem read during weaving; a read or UTF-8 failure becomes a
located error rather than a partly styled page.

--- Choose page color schemes
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
enum BuiltInColorScheme {
    Litweb,
    PrismDefault,
    Dark,
    Funky,
    Okaidia,
    Twilight,
    Coy,
    SolarizedLight,
    TomorrowNight,
}

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
enum PageColorScheme {
    Disabled,
    BuiltIn(BuiltInColorScheme),
    Custom(String),
}

impl PageColorScheme {
    fn css(&self) -> Option<&str> {
        match self {
            Self::Disabled => None,
            Self::BuiltIn(scheme) => Some(scheme.css()),
            Self::Custom(css) => Some(css),
        }
    }
}

fn last_command(commands: &[Command], kind: CommandKind) -> Option<&Command> {
    commands.iter().rev().find(|command| command.kind == kind)
}

fn page_color_scheme(
    program: &Program,
    chapter: Option<&Chapter>,
    command_line: Option<&Path>,
    errors: &mut Vec<WeaveError>,
) -> PageColorScheme {
    let result = if let Some(value) = command_line {
        resolve_color_scheme(value, None, &program.origin)
    } else {
        let command = chapter
            .and_then(|chapter| last_command(&chapter.commands, CommandKind::ColorScheme))
            .or_else(|| last_command(&program.commands, CommandKind::ColorScheme));
        match command {
            Some(command) if command.arguments.is_empty() => {
                Ok(PageColorScheme::Disabled)
            }
            Some(command) => {
                let source = Path::new(&command.origin.file);
                resolve_color_scheme(
                    Path::new(&command.arguments),
                    source.parent(),
                    &command.origin,
                )
            }
            None => Ok(PageColorScheme::BuiltIn(BuiltInColorScheme::Litweb)),
        }
    };

    match result {
        Ok(scheme) => scheme,
        Err(error) => {
            errors.push(error);
            PageColorScheme::Disabled
        }
    }
}

fn resolve_color_scheme(
    value: &Path,
    source_directory: Option<&Path>,
    origin: &SourceOrigin,
) -> Result<PageColorScheme, WeaveError> {
    if value
        .to_str()
        .is_some_and(|name| name.eq_ignore_ascii_case("none"))
    {
        return Ok(PageColorScheme::Disabled);
    }
    if value
        .extension()
        .is_some_and(|extension| extension == "css")
    {
        let path = if value.is_absolute() {
            value.to_owned()
        } else {
            source_directory
                .unwrap_or_else(|| Path::new(""))
                .join(value)
        };
        let bytes = fs::read(&path).map_err(|error| WeaveError {
            origin: origin.clone(),
            kind: WeaveErrorKind::ReadColorScheme {
                path: path.clone(),
                kind: error.kind(),
            },
        })?;
        let css = String::from_utf8(bytes).map_err(|error| WeaveError {
            origin: origin.clone(),
            kind: WeaveErrorKind::ColorSchemeInvalidUtf8 {
                path,
                valid_up_to: error.utf8_error().valid_up_to(),
            },
        })?;
        return Ok(PageColorScheme::Custom(css));
    }

    let Some(name) = value.to_str() else {
        return Err(WeaveError {
            origin: origin.clone(),
            kind: WeaveErrorKind::UnknownColorScheme {
                name: value.to_string_lossy().into_owned(),
            },
        });
    };
    let scheme = match name.to_ascii_lowercase().as_str() {
        "litweb" => BuiltInColorScheme::Litweb,
        "prism-default" => BuiltInColorScheme::PrismDefault,
        "dark" => BuiltInColorScheme::Dark,
        "funky" => BuiltInColorScheme::Funky,
        "okaidia" => BuiltInColorScheme::Okaidia,
        "twilight" => BuiltInColorScheme::Twilight,
        "coy" => BuiltInColorScheme::Coy,
        "solarized-light" => BuiltInColorScheme::SolarizedLight,
        "tomorrow-night" => BuiltInColorScheme::TomorrowNight,
        _ => {
            return Err(WeaveError {
                origin: origin.clone(),
                kind: WeaveErrorKind::UnknownColorScheme {
                    name: name.to_owned(),
                },
            });
        }
    };
    Ok(PageColorScheme::BuiltIn(scheme))
}
---

@s Parts Shared by Every Complete Page

One-file and book pages use the same safe URL and escaping operations. They
also draw from the same base styles and the same optional equation and
highlighting support. The generated Rust collects those parts in one group so
the two page forms cannot quietly choose different helpers.

--- Weaver HTML support and styles
@{Build a relative page URL}
@{Percent-encode a URL component}
@{Escape HTML text and attributes}
@{Math asset model}
@{Highlight asset model}
@{Render math page assets}
@{Render highlighting page assets}
@{Highlight theme styles}
@{Default page style}
@{Math page style}
@{Identifier index style}
@{Book navigation style}
@{Book identifier-index style}
---

@s Opening a Book HTML Document

The one-file renderer from Weaver and the book renderer below surround their
safe bodies with the same essentials: an HTML doctype, language, UTF-8
metadata, a viewport rule for small screens, an escaped title, embedded
styles, optional local asset links, and then `body` and `main`.

Book pages append navigation styles to the base stylesheet. Pages that link
the optional identifier index add its small navigation extension; only the
identifier page itself needs the identifier-list stylesheet.

@code{BookPageStyle} names those three cases so callers do not have to pass
an unclear pair of Boolean values.

--- Open a book HTML document

enum BookPageStyle {
    Ordinary,
    IdentifierNavigation,
    IdentifierIndex,
}

fn render_book_head(
    output: &mut String,
    title: &str,
    page_style: BookPageStyle,
    current_path: &Path,
    uses_math: bool,
    highlight_scheme: Option<&PageColorScheme>,
) {
    output.push_str("<!doctype html>\n");
    render_html_element_open(output, uses_math, highlight_scheme.is_some());
    output.push_str("<head>\n");
    output.push_str("<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n");
    output.push_str(
        "<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n",
    );
    output.push_str("<title>");
    push_escaped_text(output, title);
    output.push_str("</title>\n");
    if uses_math {
        render_math_stylesheet_link(output, current_path);
    }
    output.push_str("<style>\n");
    output.push_str(DEFAULT_STYLE);
    output.push_str(BOOK_STYLE);
    if uses_math {
        output.push_str(MATH_STYLE);
    }
    if matches!(
        page_style,
        BookPageStyle::IdentifierNavigation | BookPageStyle::IdentifierIndex
    ) {
        output.push_str(BOOK_IDENTIFIER_STYLE);
    }
    if matches!(page_style, BookPageStyle::IdentifierIndex) {
        output.push_str(IDENTIFIER_STYLE);
    }
    if let Some(scheme) = highlight_scheme {
        render_highlight_theme_css(output, scheme);
    }
    output.push_str("</style>\n");
    if uses_math {
        render_math_scripts(output, current_path);
    }
    if highlight_scheme.is_some() {
        render_highlight_scripts(output, current_path);
    }
    output.push_str("</head>\n<body>\n<main>\n");
}
---

@s Styling the Readable Page

The embedded base stylesheet makes the unenhanced page comfortable to read
with fonts already available in the browser. Its groups control five visible
ideas: page width and typography, continuous section openings, bordered code
and quiet relationship notes, visible keyboard focus and hidden accessible
labels, and scroll containers for material that is wider than the page.

The CSS below is production source, but its individual property values are
less important than those purposes. Semantic headings remain headings,
number-only headings retain wording for screen readers, and keyboard users
can see which link has focus. Wide code and tables scroll within their own
panels rather than widening the whole document. Inline code preserves authored
spaces while still wrapping when necessary; code blocks retain the whitespace
behavior of their surrounding `pre` element.

--- Default page style
const DEFAULT_STYLE: &str = r#"body {
    color: #222;
    background: #fff;
    font-family: Georgia, serif;
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin: 0;
}
main {
    max-width: 56rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(0.75rem, 4vw, 2rem);
}
.section {
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
}
.section-heading {
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
}
.section-opening > .section-heading,
.section-opening > p,
.section-opening > .codeblock_name {
    display: inline;
    margin: 0;
}
.section-number,
.chapter-number,
.definition-operator {
    white-space: nowrap;
}
.visually-hidden {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
}
.codeblock {
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
}
.codeblock-first {
    margin-top: 0;
}
.codeblock_name {
    margin-left: min(1rem, 3vw);
    font-family: monospace;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}
.section-opening > .codeblock_name {
    margin-left: 0;
}
.definition-operator {
    font-family: Georgia, serif;
}
code {
    white-space: pre-wrap;
}
pre code {
    white-space: inherit;
}
pre {
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid #d8d8d8;
    background: #f7f7f7;
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
    line-height: 1.35;
}
.table-scroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
}
.table-scroll table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: max-content;
    min-width: 100%;
}
.table-scroll th,
.table-scroll td {
    border: 1px solid #d8d8d8;
    padding: 0.45rem 0.7rem;
    vertical-align: top;
}
.table-scroll th {
    background: #f7f7f7;
}
.table-align-left {
    text-align: left;
}
.table-align-center {
    text-align: center;
}
.table-align-right {
    text-align: right;
}
.nocode {
    color: #555;
    font-style: italic;
}
.seealso {
    margin: 0.2rem 0 0 min(1rem, 3vw);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: #555;
}
.section-name a,
.section-reference a,
.nocode a,
.seealso a {
    text-decoration: none;
}
.section-name a:focus-visible,
.section-reference a:focus-visible,
.nocode a:focus-visible,
.seealso a:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid currentColor;
    outline-offset: 2px;
}
a {
    color: #245b8a;
}
"#;
---

@s The Versioned Math Asset Bundle

KaTeX is stored as reviewed public assets rather than pasted into this
chapter. @code{include_bytes!} places those files in the compiled @code{lw}
binary, so an installed program can emit an offline document without finding
the repository at runtime. The array below is the complete public bundle:
KaTeX's JavaScript, stylesheet, compact web-font files, Litweb's small
renderer, and the license and provenance files. The long font list belongs in
the authoritative manifest rather than in a prose inventory.

The output directory includes the KaTeX version. A later upgrade can therefore
change the bundle deliberately without making an existing page silently load
different rendering code.

--- Math asset model
const MATH_ASSET_DIRECTORY: &str = "litweb-assets/katex-0.18.1";
const INLINE_MATH_MARKER: &[u8] = b"<span class=\"litweb-math litweb-math-inline\">";
const DISPLAY_MATH_MARKER: &[u8] = b"<div class=\"litweb-math litweb-math-display\">";

struct MathAsset {
    path: &'static str,
    bytes: &'static [u8],
}

const MATH_ASSETS: &[MathAsset] = &[
